-- title:
--   Level-four Hauptmodul relation between j and λ/16
-- statement:
--   The assertion is a single closed identity in the field $\mathbb{Q}((X))$ of Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$ (`LaurentSeries ℚ`), with no variables or hypotheses. Here $\mu :=$ `lambdaModC ℚ` is the coefficientwise image under $\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Q}$ (via `laurentMap`) of the integral Laurent series `lambdaInt`, namely of $X\cdot E^{8}\cdot \mathrm{qExpand}\,4\,(E^{16})\cdot \mathrm{qExpand}\,2\,(U)$, where $E$ denotes the power series `etaProd` (normalised so that its constant coefficient is $1$), $U$ denotes `dedekindEtaUnitInv`, the multiplicative inverse of $E^{24}$, the factor $X$ is the Hahn series `HahnSeries.single 1 1`, and `qExpand ℤ n` is the variable-rescaling ring homomorphism $X\mapsto X^{n}$; and $j :=$ `jq` is $X^{-1}$ (the Hahn series `HahnSeries.single (-1) 1`) times the image in $\mathbb{Q}[[X]]$ of the integral power series `jNum`. The conclusion is the equality
--   $$j\cdot\mu\cdot(1-16\mu)^{4} \;=\; \bigl(1+224\mu+256\mu^{2}\bigr)^{3}$$
--   in $\mathbb{Q}((X))$.
--
--   This is the classical degree-six covering $X_0(4)\to X(1)$ written on $q$-expansions: the Hauptmodul $u=\eta(\tau)^8\eta(4\tau)^{16}/\eta(2\tau)^{24}=\lambda(2\tau)/16$ satisfies $j=(1+224u+256u^2)^3/\bigl(u(1-16u)^4\bigr)$. It is used to identify the Laurent-series algebra generated by $\mu$ with the base change of the modular function field of level four, and in the localisation arguments at the $\lambda$-node.
